WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.560
MARK MIRCHANDANI: If you
have a large team working

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.660
with your Google Cloud
Platform resources,

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.310
you may want to make
sure you set caps

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.967
on the amount of certain
resources that can be used.

00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:09.550
On this episode of
"Beyond Your Bill,"

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.220
we'll talk about what quotas
are and how to set them up.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.545
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.900
Budgets allow you to
trigger notifications

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.480
so that you know how your
actual and forecasted costs are

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.590
trending over time and prompt
you to take action if needed.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.110
Quotas allow you to set
your own capped limit

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.630
to the number of concurrent
resources in a project

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.240
or the number of API requests.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.090
Quotas are important to protect
the community of GCP users

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.340
and safeguard against
unforeseen spikes

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.430
in usage that could
lead to budget overruns.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:45.840
There are two types of quotas--

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.222
allocation quota that
measures that a resource,

00:00:48.222 --> 00:00:49.680
like the number of
virtual machines

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.560
you can have spun up at
once, and rate quotas

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.600
that reset after a
certain amount of time,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.040
like API requests per minute.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.120
Most resources and
APIs in each project

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.850
have quotas set by default,
and some can even be unlimited.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.129
But you can also
configure specific quotas

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:06.990
to help manage your
cloud resources

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.160
and establish cost controls.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.760
For example, let's say you
have a test environment where

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.980
a lot of users have
access to BigQuery.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.020
Since it is a test
environment, people

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.970
may be a little less
careful about optimizing

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.390
their queries, which may
result in some costly mistakes.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.640
So you may want to set
up in a quota limit

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.230
to ensure that
each user can only

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.750
query a certain amount
of data every day.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.060
Let's check out how to view
your current quota limits

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.650
and how to modify an
existing quota limit.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.460
In the GCP console, you can
use the top left navigation

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.490
to select Quotas
under IAM and Admin.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.160
Since quotas are
set per project,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.660
you may need to choose a project
if you haven't selected one

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:43.323
already.

00:01:43.323 --> 00:01:45.240
You'll also need to have
the right permissions

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.000
for the project, such as
project owner or editor,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.090
in order to change
any quota limits.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.840
The Quotas page
contains a large number

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.420
of quotas across all the
GCP products and services

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.740
available to you.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.450
There is a lot
here, so you can use

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.450
the filters to narrow down the
quotas you're interested in.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.240
Filtering by type lets you
choose between unlimited quotas

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.980
or quotas with usage.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.230
Service, metric, and
location filter down

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.509
to just quotas matching
what you select.

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:12.210
For our example, we'll start
by filtering to just BigQuery

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.770
to keep the list manageable.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.610
You can click on the
drop-down by Service,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.910
click on None to
deselect everything,

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.920
and then click on BigQuery API.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.740
Now we're just looking at
BigQuery related quotas.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.690
Each row is a
different quota and you

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.240
can click on a quota to go
to the API specific quota

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.390
page, which may show
graphs or other details.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:32.610
On our current
project-wide quota page,

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.080
each row starts
with a description

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.510
of the quota, then the
location or regions

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.160
that the quota affects.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.230
After that, the current
usage and peak usage

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.300
over the past seven
days is shown.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.340
For allocation quotas,
the current usage

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.880
is how much of the resource
is currently in use,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.630
while for rate
quotas, it's the usage

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.400
so far in the current day.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.850
The last column is the actual
limit enforced by the quota.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:57.540
The first row item, Query Usage
Per Day, shows us how much data

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.390
we've queried today in BigQuery
and that there's currently

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.190
no limit set up.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.170
If we edited this quota,
we could set a limit

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.962
on the amount of data
that could be queried

00:03:05.962 --> 00:03:08.430
for day for the entire project.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.950
For our example, we want to
limit each user on the project.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.740
So let's select the Query Usage
Per Day Per User quota instead.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.140
We'll select the check box
and then hit Edit Quotas

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.650
at the top of the page.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.675
It's also possible to edit
multiple quotas at once.

00:03:23.675 --> 00:03:25.050
On the right side
of the console,

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.050
a form will open up where
we can input our limit.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.700
Some edits may require
you to fill out

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.580
contact information and a
form to request the limit

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.497
update rather than modifying
the limit directly.

00:03:35.497 --> 00:03:37.830
Quota requests are typically
handled within two business

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.020
days and an email
confirmation is sent out

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.720
once the requested
update has been reviewed.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.480
For this quota, we can easily
lower it from unlimited

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.700
to any number we want without
needing to make a request.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.580
Let's limit each user
to half a tebibyte

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.520
of data queried since that's
the metric that BigQuery checks

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.860
for quotas and for pricing.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.540
With a quota limit
set, there will now

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.923
be a limit for each user
when running queries.

00:03:59.923 --> 00:04:02.340
One important note is that the
Quota page doesn't show you

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.170
the current usage
for every quota,

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.348
such as the per user
quota that we just edited.

00:04:06.348 --> 00:04:07.890
Check out the link
in the description

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.410
to see more about setting
up monitoring for quotas.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.990
To test out our limit, we
can run a query in BigQuery

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.660
that is projected to process
much more data than is allowed

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:16.740
by our limit.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.560
BigQuery returns back this
error showing us the quota

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.320
has been exceeded, so
the query won't run.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.060
We can also run
smaller queries up

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.100
until our quota
limit, at which point

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.147
we can't run any more
until the next day.

00:04:28.147 --> 00:04:29.730
You can set the quota
limits that best

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.650
match your desired
usage caps across many

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.310
of the GCP services.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:34.950
It's also possible
to set up monitoring

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.710
for quota metrics in Stackdriver
so that you can create

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.780
custom dashboards or alerts.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.240
Check out the Monitoring
Quota Metrics link below

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.310
to see how to set this up.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.500
Now you should be able to
view your existing quotas

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.063
and update quota limits to
protect against surprises.

00:04:49.063 --> 00:04:50.730
Check out the links
for more information

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:51.900
on working with quotas.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

